Problem: §XV of E410 begins the 'third case', the equilibrium of a thread (rod) that is unequally elastic. The rod may have varying thickness or be made of different materials. Euler had derived general intrinsic equilibrium equations for a loaded flexible or elastic line in the previous sections. In modern notation, s is arc length, φ the tangent angle, T the tension (tangential force), V the shear (normal force), and Vv the bending moment, with v the lever arm. The loads per unit length are p (tangential) and q (normal). The equations are:
(I) dT/ds + V κ = p;
(II) dV/ds − T κ = q;
(III) d(Vv)/ds = V;
where κ = dφ/ds.
The constitutive law is moment = S(s)·κ, where S(s) is the 'absolute elasticity' (modern flexural rigidity EI(s)) as a given function of arc length. In the previous case S was the constant A.
With ds taken constant, (III) gives V = d(Sκ)/ds = (S ddφ + dS dφ)/ds². The lever arm is then v = Sκ/V = S dφ ds/(S ddφ + dS dφ).
On the next page Euler substitutes into (I): dT = p ds − (S dφ ddφ + dS dφ²)/ds². He remarks that this can no longer be integrated in closed form even if ∫p ds is known. The reason is that Sκκ' + S'κ² = (Sκ²/2)' + S'κ²/2 is not an exact derivative unless S' = 0. From (II) he gets T = (S d³φ + 2dS ddφ + ddS dφ)/(ds² dφ) − q ds/dφ. Equating the derivative of this T with the first expression gives a fourth-order ODE for the curve, which he declines to write out in general. The page thus generalizes the uniform-rod equation of the previous page, 2A d³φ + A dφ³ = 2dφ∫p ds + 2q ds, to variable rigidity.
